`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"The loss charges each expert's probability at the rate of its load",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The Switch Transformer's load-balancing loss adds a small auxiliary term to the task loss ",e.jsx(s,{id:"fedus2021switch",children:"Fedus et al., 2022"}),". For ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"N"})," experts, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i"})," is expert ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"}),"'s share of the discrete selections in a batch, and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i"})," is the mean router probability of expert ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"}),": its full-softmax probability ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"p_i"}),", averaged over the batch's tokens. The loss is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_aux = α N Σ_i f_i P_i"}),", with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α"})," the auxiliary-loss weight. A batch of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"T"})," tokens with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," experts each makes ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"T·k"})," selections, and the course divides each expert's count by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"T·k"}),", so the shares sum to one. The original formulation is a top-1 result, and this ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/(T·k)"})," normalization for ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k > 1"})," is the course's choice."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The two factors are different objects, and the asymmetry is the design. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i"})," counts integer top-k assignments, so it has no derivative and backpropagation treats it as a constant. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i"})," comes out of the differentiable softmax. The product ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i P_i"})," is therefore differentiable in one factor only, and the gradient flows through ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i"})," ",e.jsx(s,{id:"fedus2021switch",children:"Fedus et al., 2022"}),"."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i"})," held fixed, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"∂L_aux/∂P_i = αN f_i"}),". Each expert's probability is charged at a rate proportional to the traffic that expert actually received. The gradient on the logits follows by composing this with the full-softmax Jacobian ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"∂p_i/∂z_j = p_i(δ_ij − p_j)"}),' of "Gradient never sees which experts were chosen". It is the full-softmax Jacobian even when the gates use ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"softmax_topk"}),", because ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i"})," is defined from the full softmax under every softmax-family gate. Softmax couples the experts, so the update moves probability mass: lowering an overloaded expert's probability raises everyone else's. Read the loss as a linear penalty on the router's probabilities, with the loads observed on the same tokens as its coefficients."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The penalty points toward uniform routing only through an approximation. Treat ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f"})," as the empirical realization of the router that produced ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P"}),", and approximate ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f ≈ P"}),". Then ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_aux ≈ αN Σ_i P_i²"}),". Under the constraint ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Σ_i P_i = 1"}),", the sum ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Σ_i P_i²"})," is smallest at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i = 1/N"})," for every ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"}),", where it equals ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/N"}),". Uniform routing therefore minimizes the approximated loss, which is the sense in which the loss encourages balance."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The approximation is a heuristic, because ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P"})," obey different constraints. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i"})," can approach one. ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i"})," cannot exceed ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/k"}),": top-k selects ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," distinct experts per token, so an expert receives at most one of each token's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," assignments. The cap limits how concentrated the count vector can be, and it fixes no ordering between the two vectors. With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"N = 4"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k = 2"}),", the count vector ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f = (0.5, 0.5, 0, 0)"})," respects the cap and is far more concentrated than a near-uniform ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P"}),". On any given step, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f ≈ P"})," can err in either direction."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The value range makes the scale of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α"})," concrete. At perfect uniformity, ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i = P_i = 1/N"})," gives ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_aux = αN · N · (1/N²) = α"}),", whatever ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," is. The other end follows exactly. Because ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Σ_i P_i = 1"}),", the sum ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Σ_i f_i P_i"})," is at most ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"max_i f_i"}),", and with the cap at most ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/k"}),". Hence ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"L_aux ≤ αN/k"}),", an exact upper bound. Reaching it would need all of the probability mass on experts at the cap. Every ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P_i"})," stays positive for finite logits, so some mass always falls on experts below the cap, and the bound is approached and never attained. It reduces to ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"αN"})," only for top-1 routing, and it depends on ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," at all because of the course's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/(T·k)"})," normalization. With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"N = 8"}),", ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k = 2"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α = 0.01"}),", the upper bound is 0.04 and perfect uniformity gives 0.01. The uniform value is a reference point, and a batch can score below it: ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"P"})," are each averaged over the batch before they are multiplied, so a batch in which the busier experts get lower mean probabilities, for example many tokens choosing one expert with little confidence and a few choosing another with high confidence, can come in under ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α"}),". Either way the term is small next to a cross-entropy of order one. It steers the router without dominating the objective."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Choosing ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α"})," is a trade-off. If it is too small, the rich-get-richer loop wins. If it is too large, the loss pushes the router toward uniform routing even where the data would justify an uneven split. When you assert loss values in a test, use the ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," you configured: asserting ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"αN"})," as the upper bound under top-8 routing fails against a correct implementation."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The statistics need a set of tokens to average over. The exercise's ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"lbl_scope"}),' setting chooses it: each micro-batch on its own, or the whole optimizer step. The reading slide "lbl_scope sets which tokens share one statistic" gives the identity that makes the step scope exact.']})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Both router losses are defined on a full softmax",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The auxiliary loss is defined over full-softmax router probabilities. The router z-loss of ST-MoE is defined on the same softmax: it adds ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"log² Z"})," to the loss, where ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"Z = Σ_i exp(z_i)"})," is the softmax normalizer over the router logits ",e.jsx(s,{id:"zoph2022stmoe",children:"Zoph et al., 2022"}),'. It is the device Week 5 applied to output logits on its slide "Match each control to its intervention point", applied here to the router to keep its logits from drifting large. Both losses therefore belong to the softmax-family gates. A normalized-sigmoid router has no full-softmax probabilities and no softmax normalizer, so pairing either loss with ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"sigmoid_norm"})," would apply a formula outside the object it was defined on. The exercise rejects that configuration instead of guessing a meaning for it. DeepSeek V3, whose gate is a normalized sigmoid, does not use z-loss."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Router precision follows from the same normalizer. The softmax normalizer and its logarithm are the quantities z-loss exists to control, and they lose accuracy fastest in low precision. This course therefore computes softmax-family routers in fp32, even inside a bf16 model. It is a stability choice for one small projection, and the mechanism stays the same."})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{title:"A selection bias steers traffic without changing the weights",accent:4,number:"13",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
`,e.jsxs(t.li,{children:["Bias added to scores for selection only ",e.jsx(s,{id:"wang2024auxfree",children:"Wang et al., 2024"})]}),`
