`]})}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Top-2 routing collapses onto two experts at half the traffic each",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The balance view simulates a router with 8 experts and top-2 routing. Each step routes 64 tokens, so it makes 128 assignments. Each bar is one expert's share of the last step's assignments, and the dashed line marks the uniform share ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/8 = 0.125"}),". At step 0 every bar is on that line, and the step counter shows how far the run has advanced. The run plays at about twenty steps per second. Changing the mode or a slider restarts it from the same seed, so every comparison starts from the same initial state."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["The dynamics are pinned, which makes them easy to reason about. Each expert has a router preference, a score that starts with a small random offset. For each token the simulation adds seeded noise to the preferences and selects the top 2. After each step every preference moves by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"η(u_i − 1/N)"}),", where ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"u_i"})," is expert ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"i"}),"'s share of that step's assignments and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"η = 0.3"})," is the simulation's rate. This one term is the feedback loop: an expert with more than the uniform share gains preference and is selected more often on the next step. With no balancing it is the only update, so any initial asymmetry grows."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["With no balancing, two experts take all the traffic, 50% each, within about ten steps, which at that speed is about half a second. Rich-get-richer feedback can concentrate traffic, and here it concentrates all of it. It stops at two experts because top-2 routing caps any single expert at half the assignments. That is the cap ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"f_i ≤ 1/k"}),' of "An auxiliary loss pushes traffic off busy experts", here ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/2"}),". The two favored experts each hold that maximum, and the other six receive nothing."]})]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"The two fixes balance the load at different places",children:[e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Both balancing modes stop the collapse. Selecting ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"aux"})," adds the toy version of the auxiliary loss's gradient: each preference also moves by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"−αN(u_i − 1/N)"}),", so the net drift is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"(η − αN)(u_i − 1/N)"}),". The sign of ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"η − αN"})," decides the outcome, and the correction wins only when ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α > η/N = 0.0375"}),". At the default ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α = 0.05"})," the coefficient is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"−0.10"}),", a restoring drift toward the uniform share. At ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α = 0.035"})," it is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"+0.02"}),", still diverging, and the run collapses to two experts at 50% by about step 100, more slowly than with no balancing. The threshold is the toy's version of the tuning trade-off of the auxiliary loss."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Selecting ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"bias"})," leaves the preference update as it is and moves a per-expert selection bias by ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"−γ·sign(u_i − 1/N)"})," instead, with ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"γ = 0.05"})," at the default. The printed biases are the readout to watch in this mode."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["In both modes the bars of a single step still jump with the batch noise. With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"aux"})," at ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α = 0.05"}),", single-step shares range from about 4% to 22% from step 20 on. With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"bias"}),", they range from about 2% to 26% over steps 201 to 400. Averaged over steps 301 to 400, each expert's share under ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"bias"})," lies between 11.7% and 13.1%, so the average load is flat while no single step is."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The two modes differ in the router's preferences. The simulation keeps them internal. The spreads quoted here, each the gap between the largest and the smallest preference, come from the same seeded run. The auxiliary loss pulls the preferences back together: their spread falls to about 0.02 and stays there. Under the bias they keep drifting apart, from a spread of about 0.2 to about 0.62 over 400 steps, and the biases grow unequal to compensate, to a spread of about 0.70. That is the selection bias's signature. It balances which experts are selected, while the router's preferences stay as unequal as the feedback loop makes them."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:['The view carries the Illustrative tag and the label "Stylized simulation". Its dynamics are chosen, with a pinned noise width and rate, so its numbers describe the widget. Convergence speed, perplexity, specialization and production values of ',e.jsx(t.code,{children:"α"})," and ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"γ"})," come from measurements on trained models, such as the specialization findings of What Do Experts Learn?"]})]})]}),e.jsxs(i,{reading:!0,title:"lbl_scope sets which tokens share one statistic",accent:6,number:"15",items:"4",children:[e.jsxs(t.ul,{children:[`
