`]}),e.jsxs(n,{title:"Departures from 1/N, differing between rows, are the signal",children:[e.jsx(t.p,{children:"The evidence matrix gives each study one card with the same four fields, Setup, Measurement, Finding and Limit, and a link to the source underneath. Reading down one card gives one study. Reading one field across the three cards shows the shape of the evidence. The Setup field differs in every card, so no two studies share a configuration. The Measurement field differs as well: expert selections per domain for Mixtral, domain routing and perplexity for Demons in the Detail, domain routing and the quality of expert subsets for EMO. The three Findings therefore answer three different questions. The Limit field is the one most often dropped when a result is repeated, so read it before you quote the Finding next to it."}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Part C of the exercise produces a measurement of the same kind, and how you read it depends on how it is built. Take the trained MoE checkpoint and run an evaluation-only forward pass over the course-owned domain shards, with no gradient and no update, so that the routing you record belongs to one fixed model. For each domain, count the routed assignments each expert received, and divide each count by that domain's total number of assignments. With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," experts per token, that total is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"k"})," times the domain's token count. The result is a matrix with one row per domain and one column per expert, and every row sums to one."]}),e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["Row normalization makes the rows comparable when the shards differ in size, and it fixes the reference value. With ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"N"})," routed experts, a row that spreads its traffic evenly holds ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/N"})," in every cell. With 32 experts that is ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"1/32 = 0.031"}),". Read the matrix one row at a time against that value. Structure means cells that depart from that value differently from one row to the next. A row whose cells all stay near ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"0.031"})," describes routing that did not separate that domain from the others. Two near-identical rows describe routing that treats that pair of domains alike. Either observation counts against domain specialization in that checkpoint."]}),e.jsx(t.p,{children:"A structured heatmap and a diffuse one are both results. Mixtral's published null finding is part of the evidence, so a diffuse heatmap from a small model trained for under thirty minutes is a valid observation about that checkpoint. The heatmap is a new measurement on a different model, scale, data and balancing configuration. It tests the specialization question at course scale."})]})]})]}),`
